## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical RHCSA skills applying to real-world multi-tenant TLS proxying, container runtimes, SNIs, firewall rules, SELinux, systemd upgrade churn, and test planning.

## Key Concepts
- Podman containers and nginx as TLS-terminating reverse proxy
- SNI-based routing, Per-tenant backends
- nftables rate limiting per tenant
- Vault integration and live cert reload
- Zero-downtime upgrades and SELinux policy

## Code Example
```
# example nginx snippet per tenant
server {
  listen 8443 ssl;
  server_name tenantA.example.com;
  ssl_certificate /vault/certs/tenantA.crt;
  ssl_certificate_key /vault/certs/tenantA.key;
  location / { proxy_pass https://10.0.3.21:8443; }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test per-tenant rate limits under surge?
- How would you roll back a failed cert rotation?

## Why This Is Asked
Explore dynamic per-tenant TLS policy reloads and zero-downtime upgrades in a real RHCSA context.

## Key Concepts
- TLS per-tenant policies, SNI, Redis-backed config, hot-reload, Podman, systemd drop-ins, SELinux, nftables

## Code Example
```nginx
# sample
http {
  include /etc/nginx/tenant_policies.conf;
  ssl_protocols TLSv1.2 TLSv1.3;
  map $tls_sni $tenant { default back; };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle Redis outages during policy reloads?
- What metrics would you collect to validate policy correctness during upgrade?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical mastery of containerized TLS termination, lifecycle upgrades, and host firewall controls in RH9.

## Key Concepts
- Podman/nginx-like TLS termination
- systemd drop-ins for zero-downtime reloads
- nftables rate limiting
- SELinux policy for container access
- certificate rotation with hot reload

## Code Example
```bash
# container run (example)
podman run -d --name proxy \
  -v /srv/certs:/etc/nginx/certs:ro \
  -p 8443:8443:Z nginx:latest
```

```bash
# systemd drop-in (ExecReload for zero downtime)
[Service]
ExecReload=/bin/kill -HUP $MAINPID
```

```bash
# minimal SELinux module (conceptual)
module proxytls 1.0;
require {
  type container_t;
  class file read; class tcp_socket name_bind; 
}
# allow container_t to read certs and bind 8443
allow container_t cert_dir_t:file read;
allow container_t self:tcp_socket name_bind;
```

```bash
# nftables (inbound 8443 and global rate limit)
table inet proxy {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0;
    tcp dport 8443 accept
  }
}
table inet limit {
  chain input {
    type filter hook input priority 0;
    limit rate 100/minutediffers
  }
}
```

```bash
# test plan (rotation and rollback)
# 1. load test 100 req/min against https://host:8443
# 2. replace files in /srv/certs/rotated with new certs
# 3. touch a trigger or run systemd reload to apply without downtime
# 4. simulate revocation by replacing with old cert and reloading
```

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges expertise with containerized TLS, mTLS, and production-grade upgrade flows on RHCSA level. Checks practical config, SELinux scoping, and firewall integration in a realistic, scalable setup.

## Key Concepts

- Podman-based TLS termination in a container
- mTLS with CA and per-client certs
- OCSP stapling for performance
- systemd-based zero-downtime upgrade
- SELinux policy for nginx_t and cert access
- nftables rulecraft for 8443 and backend access
- upgrade/test strategy including revocation

## Code Example

```nginx
server {
  listen 8443 ssl;
  ssl_certificate /mnt/certs/server.crt;
  ssl_certificate_key /mnt/certs/server.key;
  ssl_client_certificate /mnt/certs/ca.crt;
  ssl_verify_client on;
  ssl_stapling on;
  location / {
    proxy_pass http://10.0.0.20:8080;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would certificate revocation be handled and propagated to clients?
- How would you monitor upgrades and auto-recovery from a failed deployment?
